//
// The five worked examples of RFC-0015, biomathematics and genetics, and
// §S18's rule base, one after another: Hardy–Weinberg and an FDR
// correction, a primer's reverse complement and protein, Wright–Fisher
// replicates on threads, an SIR epidemic, a Leslie matrix, and rules that
// call the family's words through bridges. Run it:
//
//   bund2 demo.bund
//
// Every draw comes from a seeded generator, so a run prints the same lines
// each time, and `expected.txt` beside this file is what it must print.
//
// Operands go deepest first: the deepest operand is a formula's first, so
// `10.0 2.0 /` divides 2.0 by 10.0 and `a b -` is b − a.
//

// A FLOAT, or each FLOAT of a LIST, to `places` decimals, so that the
// printed lines do not depend on the last bits of a long computation.
:round { 1000000.0 * math.round 1000000.0 swap / } register
:roundall { list swap { round swap + } loop } register

//
// 1. Hardy–Weinberg, and an association test with FDR. `popgen.hwe`
//    answers a MAP of the χ² test and the exact test; `stat.padjust.bh`
//    corrects a LIST of p-values by Benjamini and Hochberg.
//
"-- 1. Hardy–Weinberg, and an association test with FDR" println
:hwe 298 489 213 popgen.hwe var
"exact p for 298 AA, 489 Aa, 213 aa:  " print
:hwe var? "p.exact" get round println
"χ² p for the same counts:            " print
:hwe var? "p.chisq" get round println
:ps [ 0.001 0.02 0.04 0.3 0.5 ] var
"BH-adjusted p-values:                " print
:ps var? stat.padjust.bh roundall println
"" println

//
// 2. A primer's reverse complement, and its protein under the standard
//    genetic code, NCBI's table 1.
//
"-- 2. a primer's reverse complement, and its protein" println
"reverse complement:  " print
"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGA" bio.revcomp println
"protein:             " print
"ATGGCCATTGTAATGGGCCGCTGA" 1 bio.translate println
"" println

//
// 3. Wright–Fisher replicates on threads, reproducibly. Each child VM runs
//    the LAMBDA with its seed as its only value, so it seeds its own
//    generator and draws its own path: 200 generations of a population of
//    100 from p = 0.5. A child cannot call the parent's words, so the
//    program is the lambda itself. `vm.join` answers the child's final
//    stack, whose one value is the path; its last element is the
//    frequency after 200 generations.
//
"-- 3. Wright–Fisher replicates on threads" println
:rep { rng.seed 100 0.5 200 popgen.wf } var
:hs list var
[ 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 ] {
  list + :rep var? swap vm.spawn      // [ seed ] as the child's stack
  :hs var? + :hs swap var
} loop
:finals list var
:hs var? {
  vm.join car                         // the path
  1 swap tail car                     // its last frequency
  :finals var? + :finals swap var
} loop
"final frequencies, seeds 1 to 8:  " print
:finals var? println
"seed 3 again, on a fresh thread:  " print
3 list + :rep var? swap vm.spawn vm.join car 1 swap tail car println
"" println

//
// 4. An SIR epidemic, β = 0.5 and γ = 0.1, from 1% infected, integrated by
//    Dormand–Prince with its default tolerances to t = 100. The model is a
//    lambda, t [ S I R ] -- [ dS dI dR ]. Since dS/dR = −(β/γ)·S, every
//    point of the path keeps ln(S/S₀) = −(β/γ)·(R − R₀), the relation that
//    gives an epidemic's final size; here R₀ = 0 and β/γ = 5, and the two
//    sides agree to the integrator's tolerance.
//
"-- 4. an SIR epidemic" println
:sir {                                   // t [ S I R ] -- [ dS dI dR ]
  unfold :R swap var :I swap var :S swap var drop
  :S var? :I var? * 0.5 * -1.0 * list +                      // −βSI
  :S var? :I var? * 0.5 * :I var? 0.1 * swap - swap +        // βSI − γI
  :I var? 0.1 * swap +                                       // γI
} var
:run :sir var? [ 0.99 0.01 0.0 ] 0.0 100.0 dict ode.rk45 var
:final 1 :run var? "y" get tail car var
"S, I and R at t = 100:  " print
:final var? roundall println
"ln(S/S₀) at t = 100:    " print
0.99 1 :final var? at / math.ln round println      // ln(S/0.99)
"−(β/γ)·R at t = 100:    " print
3 :final var? at -5.0 * round println
"" println

//
// 5. A Leslie matrix's growth rate and stable stage distribution. Its
//    characteristic equation is λ³ = 0.75λ + 0.28, whose real root is
//    λ = 1.013103401237989; the stable distribution is proportional to
//    (1, s₁/λ, s₁s₂/λ²), scaled to sum to 1.
//
"-- 5. a Leslie matrix" println
[ [ 0.0 1.5 0.8 ] [ 0.5 0.0 0.0 ] [ 0.0 0.7 0.0 ] ] matrix
linalg.dominant
"growth rate λ:        " print
dup "value" get round println
"stable distribution:  " print
"vector" get roundall println
"" println

//
// 6. §S18's rule base. A rule computes through a bridge: `test` keeps a
//    solution when its lambda answers true, and `is` binds a variable to
//    what its lambda answers, so every word of the family is callable from
//    a rule. A genotype is a quoted STRING: a bare `Aa` would be a
//    variable.
//
"-- 6. a rule base over the family's words" println
:snps [
  [ pvalue rs1 0.001 ] [ pvalue rs2 0.2 ] [ pvalue rs3 0.04 ]
  [ genotype ann "Aa" ] [ genotype bob "AA" ]
  [ counts l1 298 489 213 ] [ counts l2 14 57 50 ]
  [ [ significant S ] ← [ pvalue S P ] [ test { 0.05 P < } ] ]
  [ [ carrier X ] ← [ genotype X G ] [ test { G "Aa" == } ] ]
  [ [ hwe.p L P ] ← [ counts L AA Aa AAa ]
                    [ is P { AA Aa AAa popgen.hwe "p.exact" get } ] ]
] kb! var

"significant at 0.05:  " print
:snps var? [ significant S ] kb.query { :S get } map println
"carriers:             " print
:snps var? [ carrier X ] kb.query { :X get } map println
"exact HWE p by locus: " print
:snps var? [ hwe.p L P ] kb.query { :P get round } map println
